  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, I'm sorry this has dragged on, especially since you've had to ask more than once. I've checked your laptop and found the problem: the drive is almost full. Only about 9 GB of its 256 GB is free (3.5%), and that's why files won't save. Here's how to free up space quickly:\n\n1. Empty your Recycle Bin, then clear out your Downloads folder. Move anything you need to keep to OneDrive first.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Select temporary files and old Windows Update files, then click Remove. This often frees several gigabytes.\n3. In OneDrive, turn on Files On-Demand. Right-click the OneDrive cloud icon, open Settings, then go to Sync and back up > Advanced settings. This keeps your synced files in the cloud until you open them, so they stop taking up space on the laptop.\n4. Uninstall any apps you no longer use (Settings > Apps > Installed apps).\n\nWhen you're done, please reply to this ticket and I'll check the free space again. If there still isn't enough room after the cleanup, I'll send it straight to our next-level team to look at a larger drive or a replacement laptop.",
  "internal_note": "User: Leo Martins (user_id 6, Sales). Device BL-LT-0106 (Win 11 Pro) is online. Disk is 256 GB with 9 GB free (3.5%), and the device shows a low disk space warning. That is well under the 10% threshold in KB-017 and explains why files won't save. I sent the user the KB-017 cleanup steps: Recycle Bin/Downloads, temp files and old Windows Update files, OneDrive Files On-Demand, uninstalling unused apps. Next: run check_device_status again after the user replies. Escalate to Tier 2 if free space is still under 10% (the drive may be too small for this user's data). The user says this is their third request, but I didn't find the earlier tickets. Please check for duplicates and consider prioritizing because the user is very frustrated.",
